gly  the number of players who had  prescriptions for adderall  was still way below the national average  we  started testing in march of 2016  at iem katowice since then we've tested  over 260 players  primarily tier one counter-strike  players but also across a range of other  games  many players have been tested many times  particularly  in tier one counter-strike  and throughout that entire process not  only have we not had a single positive  but also we've surveyed about 400  players and the results of those surveys  are  that they raise absolutely no concern  about  doping in the top levels of professional  esport  but while cs go was working to get rid  of adderall  other esports leagues seemed less  concerned  a 2018 interview conducted by shaq news  with then commissioner of the overwatch  league nate nanzer  reaffirmed the league's stance on  adderall that there was no evidence it  would make you better at overwatch  so while illegal conduct and  performance-enhancing drugs were  prohibited by the rules there would be  no testing effort  nanzer's reply then raises an  interesting question  does adderall actually improve a  player's performance  clearly there were and are a lot of  players  that believe it does but the science is  a little less conclusive  studies have been completed about the  drug's role in traditional athletics  and in academia but i wasn't able to  find one specifically about esports  performance  in an article in pc gamer from december  2020  psychiatrist william dodson pointed out  that players that see an improvement  may simply be experiencing a placebo  effect  or even potentially have undiagnosed  adhd  but whether adderall actually helps  players reach  the peak of their potential as some  people have put it  one thing seems clear developing an  addiction to adderall can have  serious negative side effects besides  things like restlessness and dry mouth  adderall can cause increased paranoia  aggression  and hostility to say nothing of the  various rare  physical side effects and when you start  taking even more than the usual dose to  try to get the same effects  things can get ugly in my opinion that's  as good a reason as any to test for it  in every esport  to protect the players who might feel  pressured to try it  to bring their performance to some  higher level the thing is esports  continues to grow  and with it we're seeing increased  potential to make money through  competing even if adderall had  zero positive effect on players there  are clearly people out there who are  willing to  try it in order to gain that competitive  edge  and if that initial attempt becomes an  addiction  it can have destructive consequences if  all leagues tested for it then  simply trying adderall and then maybe  getting addicted to it  wouldn't be an option at all whether  it's performance enhancing or not  is kind of not the point and listen i'm  one of the last people  that's going to advocate for increased  drug testing because  well maybe you caught my joke in the  last video i did  and please don't call it the after plant  meta we already have a term  for it and after plant just sounds  really weird  in any case my experience with the after  plant meta  is ordering onion rings at 1am shout out  to my local burger place come on  it's legal here get off my back if you  live somewhere that is in canada you  might be surprised to find out that  here it's pretty rare to test new  employees for drugs  in fact you basically need to be doing a  job where safety is paramount  and like habitual drug use would cause  harm to someone else  i've literally never been tested for  drugs by an employer but we're not  talking about my job at canadian  bookstore chain  chapters in 2007 we're talking about a  competition  where the rules rightfully prohibit the  use of substances  that could affect the integrity of the  games that are being played  let's agree to protect both the  competition and the bodies and mind